Page 3 of 5

2.96.030 – Public members conflicts of interest; disclosure; reporting.

A. Every public member shall have the affirmative duty to promptly and publicly disclose the
existence of any conflict of interest or potential conflict of interest with respect to any
proposed transaction, contract, policy, program or other matter under consideration by the
public member in their official capacity. When in doubt, the public member shall disclose
matters as potential conflicts of interest and disclose all relevant facts relating to the
potential conflict.

B. If a public member declares a conflict of interest on a proposed transaction, contract,
policy, program or other matter under consideration, or if two-thirds of the board or
commission less the public member with the alleged conflict, determine that a conflict of
interest exists, the conflicted public member shall leave the meeting and any future
meetings during the times where the conflict may be discussed or considered. The absence
of the conflicted public member shall be recorded in the official minutes of the board or
commission.

C. No public member, upon the disclosure of a conflict of interest or a determination that
conflict exists in accordance with subsection (B) of this section, shall vote upon or engage
in conduct in their official capacity intended to influence or otherwise affect the outcome
of an issue being considered by the board or commission for which the public member is
conflicted.

D. If any public member has reason to believe that another public member of the same board
or commission has a conflict of interest or potential conflict of interest, the public member
with such belief shall inform the board or commission, including disclosing all relevant
facts and concerns relating thereto.

E. If any person has reason to believe that a public member presently has or previously had a
conflict of interest, the person may bring a complaint to the attendant board or commission
chairman. The board or commission chairman that received the complaint shall
acknowledge receipt of the complaint and if he or she determines the complaint has merit,
shall bring the issue to the attention of the members of the board and commission. Any
potential conflict of interest raised in accordance with this subsection shall be administered
as provided by subsection (B) of this section.

2.96.040 – Public officials; personal or private interest; abstention and votes.

A. No public official shall decide or vote on any matter in their official capacity in which the
public official or public member has a personal or private interest.

B. Any public official who has a personal or private interest in a matter under consideration
shall abstain from voting. Any abstention shall be recorded in the official records of the
governing body. An abstention under this section shall only be required in clear cases of a
personal or private interest.
```

### Page 4 of 12

```text
Page 4 of 5

C. If a public official votes on a matter in their official capacity, he or she shall decline to take
any financial ownership in the transaction, contract, arrangement, policy or other such
matter upon which he or she voted for the entirety of the time that official is a member of
city council.

D. If any person has reason to believe that a public official presently has or previously had a
conflict of interest, the person may bring a complaint to the presiding officer of the
governing body. The presiding officer shall acknowledge receipt of the complaint and if
the presiding officer determines the complaint has merit, shall bring the issue to the
attention of the members of the governing body. Any potential conflict of interest raised in
accordance with this subsection shall be administered consistent with section 2.96.030(B)
of this chapter.

2.96.050 – Misuse of office; use of title prestige of office.

A. No public official, public member or city employee shall:

1. Supervise or manage an immediate family member or domestic partner who is in
an office or position or has employment with the City of Cheyenne;

2. Advocate or cause the employment, appointment, promotion, transfer or
advancement of a family member or a domestic partner to an office or position of
the City of Cheyenne;

3. Use his or her office or position for their own private benefit or use public funds,
time, personnel, facilities or equipment for his or her private benefit or that of
another person or entity unless otherwise authorized by law.

2.96.060 – Violations by public members, public officials or city employees.

A violation of any provision of this chapter may constitute sufficient cause for termination of
a person's employment with the City of Cheyenne, for the censure of a public official or public
member or the removal of a public official or public member from his or her office or position.

Section 2.  Sections, paragraphs, sentences, clauses, and phrases of this ordinance are
severable. If any section, paragraph, sentence, clause, or phrase of this ordinance is declared
unconstitutional or otherwise unlawful, such declaration shall not affect any of the remaining
sections, paragraphs, sentences, clauses, and phrases.

Section 3.  This ordinance shall be in full force and effect upon approval and publication.

